1. Traversal Algorithms
DFS (Depth First Search)

BFS (Breadth First Search)



2. Shortest Path Algorithms
Dijkstra’s Algorithm (single source shortest path for non-negative weights)

Bellman-Ford Algorithm (handles negative weights)

Floyd-Warshall Algorithm (all pairs shortest paths)

Johnson’s Algorithm (all pairs shortest paths, efficient for sparse graphs)

Yen’s Algorithm (find the K-shortest paths)

Eppstein’s Algorithm (find the K-shortest paths)

A* Search (heuristic-based shortest path, AI applications)

BFS for Unweighted Shortest Path

0-1 BFS (for edge weights 0 or 1, uses deque)



3. Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) Algorithms
Kruskal’s Algorithm (uses Union-Find / Disjoint Set)

Prim’s Algorithm (uses greedy + priority queue)

Boruvka’s Algorithm (less common, but useful in parallel/distributed systems)



4. Topological Ordering / DAG Algorithms
Topological Sort (DFS-based)

Kahn’s Algorithm (BFS-based)

Longest Path in DAG (using topological order)



5. Connectivity Algorithms
Union-Find / Disjoint Set Union (DSU)

Kosaraju’s Algorithm (strongly connected components, SCC)

Tarjan’s Algorithm (SCC + articulation points + bridges)

Fleury’s / Hierholzer’s Algorithm (Eulerian path/circuit)

Hamiltonian Path / Circuit (NP-complete)



6. Flow / Matching Algorithms
Ford-Fulkerson Algorithm (max flow)

Edmonds-Karp Algorithm (max flow with BFS)

Dinic’s Algorithm (faster max flow)

Hopcroft–Karp Algorithm (maximum bipartite matching)

Hungarian Algorithm (assignment problem, bipartite weighted matching)


7. Cycle Detection
Cycle Detection in Undirected Graph (DFS / Union-Find)

Cycle Detection in Directed Graph (DFS with recursion stack / Kahn’s Algo)


8. Matching / Bipartite Graph
Bipartite Check (using BFS/DFS coloring)

Hopcroft-Karp Algorithm, König’s theorem (Maximum Bipartite Matching)

Hungarian Algorithm (Assignment Problem)

Ford–Fulkerson, Edmonds–Karp, Dinic's Algorithm



9. Eulerian & Hamiltonian Paths
Eulerian Path & Circuit (Hierholzer’s Algorithm)

Hamiltonian Path/Cycle (NP-Complete, backtracking/DP approaches)



10. Advanced / Specialized Algorithms
Johnson’s Algorithm (for sparse graphs, APSP)

Warshall’s Algorithm (transitive closure)

PageRank (Google search algorithm) (graph ranking)

Minimum Cut / Maximum Flow (Stoer–Wagner, Gomory-Hu Tree)

    Transitive Closure (Floyd-Warshall)

Warshall’s Algorithm (reachability matrix)

Graph Coloring

Articulation Points & Bridges (Tarjan’s Algorithm)

Biconnected Components

Chordal Graph Recognition

Planarity Testing





? In Interviews (Google, Meta, Amazon, Microsoft, etc.), the most commonly asked are:
BFS, DFS

Topological Sort (DFS & Kahn’s)

Dijkstra & Bellman-Ford

Kruskal & Prim (MST)

Union-Find (Cycle detection, Connected Components)

Tarjan / Kosaraju (SCCs)

Graph Traversals + Applications (bipartite check, connected components, shortest path in unweighted graphs, etc.)

Tree Algorithms:

    Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) – Binary Lifting, Euler Tour + RMQ

    Heavy-Light Decomposition

    Centroid Decomposition

Graph Decomposition:

    Bridge Tree

    Block-Cut Tree

Randomized Graph Algorithms

Dynamic Graph Algorithms (incremental/decremental connectivity, union-find with rollback)




----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



1. Greedy Coloring (Sequential Coloring)

Idea: Assign the smallest possible color to each vertex sequentially.

Time Complexity: O(V + E)

Use Case: Quick heuristic; works for general graphs.

Notes: May not give minimum colors (chromatic number), but fast and simple.

2. Welsh-Powell Algorithm

Idea: Order vertices by decreasing degree, then apply greedy coloring.

Time Complexity: O(V²)

Use Case: Better than simple greedy for reducing colors.

3. Backtracking (Exact Coloring)

Idea: DFS + try all colors for each vertex ? backtrack if conflict.

Time Complexity: O(k^V) for k colors (NP-hard)

Use Case: Small graphs / exact chromatic number computation

Notes: Only feasible for small graphs in interviews.

4. DSATUR (Degree of Saturation)

Idea: Always pick the vertex with the highest number of differently colored neighbors next.

Time Complexity: O(V²)

Use Case: Heuristic to reduce colors; often better than greedy.

5. Bipartite Check Coloring

Idea: 2-coloring check using BFS/DFS.

Time Complexity: O(V + E)

Use Case: For bipartite graphs ? simple 2-color problem.

6. Coloring for Special Graphs

Tree Coloring ? Always 2-colorable

Cycle Coloring ? 2-colorable if even, 3-colorable if odd

Planar Graphs ? 4-color theorem (interview theory)

7. Map Coloring / Constraint Satisfaction (CSP)

Idea: Backtracking + constraints, often used in scheduling, Sudoku-like problems.

Time Complexity: Exponential in general

Use Case: FAANG interviews for problem-solving + backtracking

8. Interval Graph Coloring

Idea: Sort intervals by start time, assign colors (rooms).

Time Complexity: O(V log V)

Use Case: Real-world scheduling problems

9. Topological / DAG-based Coloring

Idea: Use topological order and assign colors for precedence constraints

Time Complexity: O(V + E)

Use Case: Task scheduling, layered coloring